1760ae1c20" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89017559-2077-57e8-85e1-d0eabb36b51a" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=5 \\\\ -8x - 2y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&(-3x - y = 5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ &-8x - 2y = 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 2y &= -10 \\\\\n-8x - 2y &= 3 \\\\\n(6x - 8x) &= (-10 + 3) \\\\\n-2x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e7bfcbe-4913-53e0-98c7-171965adfe62" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1492c2d9-3b96-5d99-af2f-6e35