} :question/id #uuid "997e6dc4-14e3-5976-b4b9-16bbe485f4b0" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - 2y &=5 \\\\ -8x - 4y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-4$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&(-3x - 2y = 5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ &-8x - 4y = 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 4y &= -10 \\\\\n-8x - 4y &= 3 \\\\\n(6x - 8x) &= (-10 + 3) \\\\\n-2x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e7bfcbe-4913-53e0-98c7-171965adfe62" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e65d3b-c190-5cd9-8a2d-a34b04008864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508254} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cfa321-7684-5b8e-9a83-5671a3ae619b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/id